ACPI: PCI: find the _PRT of a bridge that has a device-tree node

openHC's EA boards (Intel CE5300) boot with ACPI and link a device tree into
the kernel (patch 0015) to describe board devices behind the CE5300 A/V bridge
00:01.0: GPIO hogs and names, the I2C devices, the SPI switch, the USB VBUS
supply. Bus 1 only gets an OF node through that bridge's node, so the bridge
must be in the tree -- and then pci_set_of_node() gives it an OF fwnode,
pci_set_acpi_fwnode() skips attaching its ACPI companion, and
acpi_pci_irq_find_prt_entry() finds no handle for bus 1. Every device on it
comes up "PCI INT x: no GSI" (measured on an EA3: gpio, spi, i2c, e1000,
sdhci, ehci, the i2s), so the eMMC times out, eth0 has no carrier and SPI
transfers never complete.

The bridge's namespace node and its _PRT are still there. When a bus's bridge
has no companion, look that node up under the parent bus's node by _ADR -- the
same lookup acpi_pci_find_companion() does -- and use its _PRT. Nothing changes
for a bridge that has a companion, which is every ACPI-only system.

Author: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
---
--- a/drivers/acpi/pci_irq.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/pci_irq.c
@@ -195,6 +195,32 @@
 	*entry_ptr = entry;
 
 	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The ACPI node that holds the _PRT for devices on @bus: normally the ACPI
+ * companion of the bus's bridge. A PCI-PCI bridge described in a device tree
+ * (a built-in DT alongside ACPI, hanging board devices off the PCI functions
+ * behind it) has an OF fwnode instead, so pci_set_acpi_fwnode() never attached
+ * its companion -- yet its namespace node, and the _PRT under it, still exist.
+ * Find it under the parent bus's node by _ADR, as acpi_pci_find_companion()
+ * would have.
+ */
+static struct acpi_device *acpi_pci_bus_prt_node(struct pci_bus *bus)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *bridge = bus->self;
+	struct acpi_device *parent;
+
+	if (!bus->bridge)
+		return NULL;
+	if (ACPI_COMPANION(bus->bridge) || !bridge)
+		return ACPI_COMPANION(bus->bridge);
+
+	parent = acpi_pci_bus_prt_node(bridge->bus);
+	if (!parent)
+		return NULL;
+	return acpi_find_child_device(parent, (PCI_SLOT(bridge->devfn) << 16) |
+				      PCI_FUNC(bridge->devfn), true);
 }
 
 static int acpi_pci_irq_find_prt_entry(struct pci_dev *dev,
@@ -203,11 +229,9 @@
 	acpi_status status;
 	struct acpi_buffer buffer = { ACPI_ALLOCATE_BUFFER, NULL };
 	struct acpi_pci_routing_table *entry;
-	acpi_handle handle = NULL;
+	acpi_handle handle;
 
-	if (dev->bus->bridge)
-		handle = ACPI_HANDLE(dev->bus->bridge);
-
+	handle = acpi_device_handle(acpi_pci_bus_prt_node(dev->bus));
 	if (!handle)
 		return -ENODEV;
 
